= <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span> Conference

# reference: Evans, Ellen L. “Adam Stegerwald and the Role of the Christian Trade Unions in the Weimar Republic.” The Catholic Historical Review 59, no. 4 (1974): 602–26.

Officially due to health reasons, and unofficially due to increased party divisions since the outcome of the 1928 elections, Wilhelm Marx has announced his intention to resign from leadership of the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. While the Executive Council has recommended Adam Stegerwald for the position, the National Committee has dithered on his appointment. With a great level of disdain for Stegerwald amongst many delegates in the National Committee, there is no guarantee that he will make it through.

On the other hand, there is Joseph Joos, whose concilliatory nature endears him to most of the party, as opposed to Stegerwald's more abrasive personality. However, if the vote is deadlocked, a dark horse could emerge as the chairman of the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. 

- @cvp_stegerwald: Adam Stegerwald has been confirmed by the rest of the party.
- @stegerwald: Adam Stegerwald has been confirmed by the rest of the party. 
- @ulitzka: The party finds an alternative in Carl Ulitzka.
- @fail: The party fails to find a suitable successsor

@cvp_stegerwald
unavailable-subtitle: [? if labor_dissent > 15: The white collar unions are still unhappy with Stegerwald's decision to oppose wage increases last year. ?][? if labor_strength < 25: Stegerwald does not have enough influence to secure the nomination. ?][? if dnvp_relation < 55 : His policy of cooperation with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> is unpopular right now. ?]
subtitle: Stegerwald sits on the right of the labor faction and would rather work with the political right than the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>.
view-if: clerical_relation >= 6 
         and labor_strength > 25 
         and dnvp_relation >= 55 
         and labor_strength >= right_strength
on-arrival: z_party_leader = "Stegerwald"; spd_relation -= 5; dnvp_relation += 8; workers_z += 1*(1-dissent); new_middle_z -= 1; cvp_stegerwald += 1; z_ideology = "Christian Corporatism"

Although Stegerwald’s opposition to last year’s civil servant raise was expected to torpedo his candidacy, his able leadership of the Labor faction since then has convinced the powerful civil servants lobby to set aside their grievances. Despite his close ties to the Christian Labor Movement, his advocacy of corporate pluralism as well as his conservative credentials have managed to mollify naysayers worried that his leadership might entail a pivot away from agrarian and industrial interests. While the <span style="color: #FF7F00;">Christian Left</span> and clerical delegation have remained cool to his candidacy, the growing influence of labor within the party and a general sentiment for change has resulted in his emergence as the frontrunner. 

With the National Committee’s support, Stegerwald has managed to cement a broad mandate to lead the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. A strong proponent of interconfessionalism, he has managed to secure support for a new party program based on his 1920 Essen Program, with the goal of turning the<span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span into a ‘Christian’, ‘National’ and ‘Social’ party,  expanding the predominantly Catholic party to Protestants too. While the move has faced extensive resistance from many in the <span style="color: #FF7F00;">Christian Left</span>, who are worried about a rightward shift for the party, Stegerwald’s close ties with the clergy have deprived Joos and Wirth of their natural ally against ‘leaving the tower’. 

As a part of these efforts, overtures have been made to the predominately Protestant <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, which Stegerwald regards as a natural ally to his efforts to build a broad Christian coalition on the right. [? if dnvp_leader = “Triumvirate” : However, his attempts at forming a united Christian party will have to be put on hold for now. With internal turmoil in the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> and constrained by the Triumvirate’s power-sharing agreement, a change of leadership will be needed for a coherent response towards Stegerwald’s overtures. ?] [? if dnvp_leader = “Hergt” : However, his attempts at forming a united Christian party will have to be put on hold for now. With internal turmoil in the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> and Hergt’s ties to old-line Prussian Conservatism, a change of leadership will be needed for a more amenable response towards Stegerwald’s overtures. ?] [? if dnvp_leader = “Hugenberg” : As a part of these efforts, overtures have been made to the moderate Protestants of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> who have been turned away by Hugenberg’s increasingly radical rhetoric. However, his attempts at forming a united Christian party will have to be put on hold for now. With the more moderate elements of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> being increasingly marginalised in the party, only through a party split can Stegerwald continue to further his goal of conservative consolidation. ?]

