Although Stegerwald’s opposition to last year’s civil servant raise was expected to torpedo his candidacy, his able leadership of the Labor faction since then has convinced the powerful civil servants lobby to set aside their grievances. Despite his close ties to the Christian Labor Movement, his advocacy of corporate pluralism as well as his conservative credentials have managed to mollify naysayers worried that his leadership might entail a pivot away from agrarian and industrial interests. While the <span style="color: #FF7F00;">Christian Left</span> and clerical delegation have remained cool to his candidacy, the growing influence of labor within the party and a general sentiment for change has resulted in his emergence as the frontrunner. 


With the National Committee’s support, Stegerwald has managed to secure a mandate to lead the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. Despite being a strong proponent of interconfessionalism, proposed reforms based on his 1920 Essen Program have been nixed. Without a sufficiently strong position in the party,  any move towards ‘leaving the tower’ has faced extensive resistance from many in the <span style="color: #FF7F00;">Christian Left</span>, who are worried about a rightward shift for the party, while the clergy remain unwilling to compromise the Catholic character of the <span style="color: #000000;">**Center Party**</span>. [? if right_strength >=  labor_strength : Furthermore, the <span style="color: #000000;">Catholic Conservatives</span> have remained divided on the issue of ‘leaving the tower’, with most preferring to strengthen their ties with the German Right instead. ?] As such, Stegerwald can only secure support for a rightward shift of the party towards ties with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, while leaving the door open for cooperation with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>.

